## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  function quickSort3Way(lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo < 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }
    if (depth > maxDepth) {
      mergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = partition3Way(arr, lo, hi);
    quickSort3Way(lo, lt - 1, depth + 1);
    quickSort3Way(gt + 1, hi, depth + 1);
  }

  quickSort3Way(0, n - 1, 0);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of Trie data structures, deduplication strategies, and efficient prefix queries—essential skills for building autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node subtree counts for O(1) prefix counting
- Deduplication via global set to maintain word uniqueness
- Prefix-based retrieval with controlled result limits
- Lexicographic ordering through systematic child traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isWord = false;
    this.passCount = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordSet = new Set();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (this.wordSet.has(word)) return;
    this.wordSet.add(word);
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.passCount++;
    }
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.passCount;
  }

  listWithPrefix(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    const result = [];
    this.dfs(node, prefix, result, limit);
    return result;
  }

  dfs(node, current, result, limit) {
    if (result.length >= limit) return;
    if (node.isWord) {
      result.push(current);
    }
    const sortedKeys = Array.from(node.next.keys()).sort();
    for (const char of sortedKeys) {
      this.dfs(node.next.get(char), current + char, result, limit);
    }
  }
}
```

